      {
        "id": "p52-q18-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "What happens to $y$, $y'$ and $y''$ as $x\\to\\infty$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "They all tend towards $-\\infty$, meaning the curve is decreasing at an increasing rate below the $x$-axis.",
          "worked": "As $x\\to\\infty$, $e^x\\to\\infty$, while $1-x$, $-x$ and $-(x+1)$ are negative with increasing magnitude. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}(1-x)e^x&\\to-\\infty\\\\-xe^x&\\to-\\infty\\\\-(x+1)e^x&\\to-\\infty.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $y$, $y'$ and $y''$ all tend to $-\\infty$: the curve lies below the $x$-axis for sufficiently large $x$ and decreases increasingly steeply.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p52-q18-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Given that $y\\to0$ as $x\\to-\\infty$, sketch the graph and write down its range.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Range: $y\\le1$.",
          "worked": "The curve approaches $y=0$ from above as $x\\to-\\infty$. It increases through the point of inflection $(-1,2e^{-1})$ to the maximum $(0,1)$, then decreases through $(1,0)$ and tends to $-\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nThe maximum value is $1$ and there is no lower bound, so the range is $(-\\infty,1]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p52-q18-answer-graph",
